WEBVTT

1
. --> .
Before we touch the worked example, notice the trap that a calculator creates.

2
. --> .
It can print a long string of digits, but that does not mean all of those digits belong in your final physics answer.

3
. --> .
Significant figures exist to stop you from pretending that a measurement is more exact than it really is.

4
. --> .
A student who copies every displayed digit is mixing mathematical output with experimental evidence, and in physics the evidence always wins.

5
. --> .
So this lesson is not about decorative rounding.

6
. --> .
It is about reporting only the precision that the original measurement can honestly support.

7
. --> .
Start with the basic counting rule.

8
. --> .
Non-zero digits count.

9
. --> .
Leading zeros do not count because they only place the decimal point.

10
. --> .
Zeros between non-zero digits do count, and trailing zeros after a decimal usually show real precision.

11
. --> .
The safest mental move is to begin counting from the first non-zero digit, not from the front of the number.

12
. --> .
That one habit protects you in tiny values like zero point zero zero four five zero and in longer numbers filled with place-holding zeros.

13
. --> .
Zeros are where students often lose confidence, so slow the logic down.

14
. --> .
In zero point zero zero four five zero, the zeros before the four only move the decimal point into place.

15
. --> .
They do not add precision.

16
. --> .
But the trailing zero after the five does matter because it shows that the reading was recorded to that level.

17
. --> .
So zero point zero zero four five zero has three significant figures, not two and not five.

18
. --> .
If you explain the job each zero is doing, you usually know whether it should be counted.

19
. --> .
Now bring in the rounding move.

20
. --> .
First decide how many significant figures or decimal places you want to keep.

21
. --> .
Then stop at the last digit you plan to keep and check the next digit only.

22
. --> .
If that next digit is four or less, the kept digit stays as it is.

23
. --> .
If the next digit is five or more, you round the kept digit up.

24
. --> .
So in twelve point three four nine to three significant figures, you keep one, two, and three, then look at the four.

25
. --> .
Because the next digit is less than five, the answer becomes twelve point three.

26
. --> .
You do not need to stare at every later digit once that immediate rounding decision has been made.

27
. --> .
The lesson then splits the reporting rules by operation type.

28
. --> .
For addition and subtraction, the final answer usually follows the fewest decimal places among the measurements.

29
. --> .
For multiplication and division, the final answer usually follows the fewest significant figures among the measurements.

30
. --> .
So twelve point three four plus one point two becomes thirteen point five because the weaker input had one decimal place.

31
. --> .
But two point five times three point four two becomes eight point six because the weaker input had two significant figures.

32
. --> .
This is why two calculations can be done on the same calculator, yet still need different reporting rules at the end.

33
. --> .
This is why a calculator display needs a final check.

34
. --> .
It may show many more digits than the measurements justify.

35
. --> .
Those extra digits are fine as working digits while you calculate, but they do not all belong in the notebook.

36
. --> .
Scientists often keep those extra digits during the middle of a calculation so they do not round too early and lose accuracy.

37
. --> .
So the scientific habit is calculate first, then trim the result using the correct rule for the operation you used.

38
. --> .
Use two anchors to keep the rules straight.

39
. --> .
First, twelve point three four plus one point two.

40
. --> .
Addition follows decimal places, so the answer keeps one decimal place and becomes thirteen point five.

41
. --> .
Second, two point five times three point four two.

42
. --> .
Multiplication follows significant figures, so the answer keeps two significant figures and becomes eight point six.

43
. --> .
Notice that both raw calculator answers carry more detail than the final reported answers.

44
. --> .
Once students hold those two examples side by side, the rule shift becomes easier to remember.

45
. --> .
Pause and check your own thinking.

46
. --> .
How many significant figures are in zero point zero zero four five zero?

47
. --> .
Three.

48
. --> .
Round twelve point three four nine to three significant figures.

49
. --> .
Twelve point three.

50
. --> .
And which rule controls two point five times three point four two?

51
. --> .
The least significant figures rule controls that result.

52
. --> .
If any one of those felt shaky, return to the zero rules and the operation split before moving on.

53
. --> .
You are now ready for the worked example in this lesson.

54
. --> .
The guided example starts with four point five six zero seven grams and asks for three significant figures.

55
. --> .
Read it in a clean sequence.

56
. --> .
First, count from the first non-zero digit so the kept figures are four, five, and six.

57
. --> .
Second, look only at the next digit, which is zero.

58
. --> .
Because that next digit is less than five, the six stays unchanged.

59
. --> .
So the final answer becomes four point five six grams.

60
. --> .
That same chain works on later questions too: choose the target precision, lock the kept digits, then let the next digit make the rounding decision.

61
. --> .
If that chain already feels clear, the worked example will feel like confirmation rather than a surprise.
